/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Let u(z) and v(z) be the deflections of the centroid in the +x and +y directions.
   - u″ and v″ are the curvatures (1/mm) in the xz and yz planes; Mx, My are in N·mm with this unit's sign convention; E is in N/mm²; I in mm⁴.
   - Comparing with the stress formula σ = k₁ x + k₂ y shows u″ = −k₁/E and v″ = −k₂/E.
   - Integrate each curvature twice along the span, as for an ordinary beam.
   - The second route resolves the load instead of the curvature.
   - Other supports change only the coefficient (L³/48 for a central load on a simple span, L⁴/8 per unit load for a uniform load on a cantilever).
   - When the load or section varies along the span, compute Mx(z) and My(z) at many stations, turn them into u″ and v″, and integrate twice numerically.
*/
